1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:01,258
Namaste.

2
00:00:01,258 --> 00:00:04,869
I'm Siddhesh Nikam, and this is the pitch for my final project.

3
00:00:04,869 --> 00:00:07,275
Lots of tools will answer a data question.

4
00:00:07,275 --> 00:00:08,708
My question is different.

5
00:00:08,708 --> 00:00:10,542
Can I prove the answer is right?

6
00:00:10,542 --> 00:00:11,218
Three terms.

7
00:00:11,218 --> 00:00:12,626
ROAS, return on ad spend:

8
00:00:12,626 --> 00:00:14,710
revenue divided by what the ads cost.

9
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:15,723
A prompt contract:

10
00:00:15,723 --> 00:00:19,779
the fixed rules I give Claude about what it may write, and in what form.

11
00:00:19,779 --> 00:00:20,624
And a test set:

12
00:00:20,624 --> 00:00:24,792
questions whose right answers I work out by hand, before Claude sees them.

13
00:00:24,792 --> 00:00:25,551
One sentence.

14
00:00:25,551 --> 00:00:30,279
CheckedSQL will let a marketing analyst ask a campaign question in plain English,

15
00:00:30,279 --> 00:00:32,964
have Claude write the SQL, run it on the data,

16
00:00:32,964 --> 00:00:36,875
and show the answer beside the query, scored against an answer key.

17
00:00:36,875 --> 00:00:40,985
When it's done, it will be a GitHub repository, and part of it already runs.

18
00:00:40,985 --> 00:00:45,095
On October twenty-fourth, with Claude's help, I built the synthetic dataset,

19
00:00:45,095 --> 00:00:48,069
ten test questions with an answer key, and the checker.

20
00:00:48,069 --> 00:00:48,826
Still to come:

21
00:00:48,826 --> 00:00:52,125
the prompt contract, the Claude step, and the evaluation log.

22
00:00:52,125 --> 00:00:53,345
Why would anyone care?

23
00:00:53,345 --> 00:00:55,728
The first alternative is a fixed dashboard,

24
00:00:55,728 --> 00:00:59,055
like the Tableau dashboard in my marketing campaign project,

25
00:00:59,055 --> 00:01:00,496
which I made with AI help.

26
00:01:00,496 --> 00:01:02,935
It answers the questions planned in advance.

27
00:01:02,935 --> 00:01:05,208
A new question means building a new view.

28
00:01:05,208 --> 00:01:07,333
The second is a general chat assistant.

29
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:08,696
On October twenty-fourth,

30
00:01:08,696 --> 00:01:12,891
I gave ChatGPT my campaign data and asked which channel had the highest ROAS.

31
00:01:12,891 --> 00:01:14,798
It answered Search, which is right.

32
00:01:14,798 --> 00:01:16,487
But there is no fixed test set,

33
00:01:16,487 --> 00:01:21,010
so no record of how often it is right on my questions, or which ones it gets wrong.

34
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:24,333
CheckedSQL runs every answer, shows its query, and scores it.

35
00:01:24,333 --> 00:01:25,185
Who opens it?

36
00:01:25,185 --> 00:01:27,869
A hiring manager for a data analyst role.

37
00:01:27,869 --> 00:01:32,190
In the first sixty seconds, they open the README, run one command,

38
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:35,006
python3 checker .py, and see a score table.

39
00:01:35,006 --> 00:01:37,167
That command already works today.

40
00:01:37,167 --> 00:01:38,207
What will it prove?

41
00:01:38,207 --> 00:01:42,367
That I can get marketing metrics right and check them against an answer key.

42
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:43,681
And three course skills:

43
00:01:43,681 --> 00:01:48,224
a prompt contract, verification against known answers, and evaluation I can repeat.

44
00:01:48,224 --> 00:01:51,016
Verification and evaluation now have real evidence:

45
00:01:51,016 --> 00:01:52,767
the checker and its score table.

46
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:53,917
The contract is next.

47
00:01:53,917 --> 00:01:54,569
What's out:

48
00:01:54,569 --> 00:01:56,585
it will never change the database.

49
00:01:56,585 --> 00:02:00,557
The checker already refuses anything but a single read-only SELECT.

50
00:02:00,557 --> 00:02:03,640
One schema, no dashboards, and no real company data:

51
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:05,833
these twenty-four rows are synthetic.

52
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:09,042
What came first was the baseline, before any Claude call:

53
00:02:09,042 --> 00:02:12,195
ten questions, an answer key worked out from the totals,

54
00:02:12,195 --> 00:02:14,277
and baseline SQL drafted with Claude.

55
00:02:14,277 --> 00:02:16,248
The checker printed ten out of ten.

56
00:02:16,248 --> 00:02:20,357
Then I added two deliberately wrong queries that average ROAS row by row.

57
00:02:20,357 --> 00:02:21,708
The checker failed both.

58
00:02:21,708 --> 00:02:22,783
What could sink it?

59
00:02:22,783 --> 00:02:24,874
An answer that looks right but isn't.

60
00:02:24,874 --> 00:02:26,797
These are Email's eight real rows.

61
00:02:26,797 --> 00:02:31,037
Four of them spend almost nothing and return fourteen to twenty times that.

62
00:02:31,037 --> 00:02:35,333
Average the row ratios, and Email's ROAS comes out at nine point seven five.

63
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:39,657
Divide total revenue by total spend instead, and Email is two point two nine.

64
00:02:39,657 --> 00:02:41,790
The best channel is Search, not Email.

65
00:02:41,790 --> 00:02:43,362
Both numbers look plausible.

66
00:02:43,362 --> 00:02:43,755
My fix:

67
00:02:43,755 --> 00:02:46,563
the metric definition goes in the prompt contract,

68
00:02:46,563 --> 00:02:51,167
and the test set has ratio questions, so this mistake gets caught, as it just was.

69
00:02:51,167 --> 00:02:52,098
Why this project?

70
00:02:52,098 --> 00:02:52,975
Mostly to learn.

71
00:02:52,975 --> 00:02:56,977
My earlier marketing campaign repository was built with a lot of AI help,

72
00:02:56,977 --> 00:02:58,950
so it shows the domain I care about,

73
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:01,965
metrics like click-through rate and return on ad spend,

74
00:03:01,965 --> 00:03:03,993
more than it proves I can code alone.

75
00:03:03,993 --> 00:03:04,541
New to me:

76
00:03:04,541 --> 00:03:08,816
writing and checking SQL myself, calling Claude from Python, prompt contracts,

77
00:03:08,816 --> 00:03:09,639
and evaluation.

78
00:03:09,639 --> 00:03:11,667
This project is how I'll prove those.

79
00:03:11,667 --> 00:03:12,763
So, what exists today:

80
00:03:12,763 --> 00:03:16,450
my two earlier repositories, this film, and the first piece of CheckedSQL:

81
00:03:16,450 --> 00:03:19,440
the dataset, the questions, the answer key, and the checker.

82
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:20,237
What will exist:

83
00:03:20,237 --> 00:03:24,173
the prompt contract, the Claude step, thirty questions, and the evaluation log.

84
00:03:24,173 --> 00:03:26,664
What I don't know yet is whether thirty is enough.

85
00:03:26,664 --> 00:03:30,750
I'll find out by watching whether new questions keep finding new kinds of failure.

86
00:03:30,750 --> 00:03:31,431
CheckedSQL:

87
00:03:31,431 --> 00:03:31,927
A Pitch.

88
00:03:31,927 --> 00:03:33,042
By Siddhesh Nikam.
